prompt:
Create a 3D interactive aquarium using Three.js. Load fish and stork models, with a school of fish swimming in the water and the stork able to dive to catch fish.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import through ES Modules in <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { GLTFLoader } from 'three/addons/loaders/GLTFLoader.js';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Model Loading]
(1) Use GLTFLoader to load assets/BarramundiFish.glb (fish model) and assets/Fox.glb (stork model);
(2) After the fish model is loaded, create 8 clones and scale them to an appropriate size (scale approximately 5);
(3) Scale the stork model to approximately 0.02 and place it on a rock above the water surface;
(4) After everything has loaded, set window.__3D_STATE__.modelLoaded = true;

[Scene Layout]
(5) Aquarium area: a transparent blue rectangular cuboid frame (wireframe mode), with dimensions 16x6x10 and centered at (0, -1, 0);
(6) Water surface: a semi-transparent horizontal plane (PlaneGeometry, blue 0x4488ff, opacity=0.3), positioned at y=2;
(7) Place a rock above the water surface (several merged spheres/cuboids, gray), with the stork standing on it at (6, 3, 0);
(8) Add a sand-and-gravel floor at the bottom (light brown plane at y=-4) and several decorative rocks;
(9) Set the scene background to dark blue (0x001133);
(10) Use blue-toned underwater lighting (blue AmbientLight + white DirectionalLight);

[Fish School Movement]
(11) 8 fish swim within the aquarium bounds;
(12) Use a combination of sine waves for each fish's movement:
  - x direction: x = centerX + amplitudeX * sin(time * speedX + phaseX)
  - y direction: y = centerY + amplitudeY * sin(time * speedY + phaseY)
  - z direction: z = centerZ + amplitudeZ * sin(time * speedZ + phaseZ)
(13) Randomly generate each fish's parameters (center, amplitude, speed, and phase), while keeping the fish within the aquarium bounds;
(14) Orient each fish to face its direction of movement (adjust rotation based on the velocity vector);
(15) Add a subtle side-to-side body-sway animation to each fish (a tail-swing effect that may be implemented using a small oscillation of rotation.y);

[Stork Interaction]
(16) Clicking the stork triggers the "dive to catch fish" action;
(17) Dive sequence:
  a) The stork finds the nearest fish;
  b) The stork takes off from the rock and flies along a parabolic path toward the target fish's position (animation duration approximately 1.5 seconds);
  c) When the stork reaches the target fish's position, that fish disappears (is caught), and fishCount -= 1;
  d) The stork returns to the rock along a parabolic path (1.5 seconds);
  e) diveCount += 1;
(18) The stork cannot be clicked again while it is diving;
(19) The caught fish respawns at a random position after 3 seconds, and fishCount += 1;

[OrbitControls]
(20) OrbitControls allows the user to adjust the viewing angle;

[HUD Display]
(21) HUD in the upper-left corner:
  - Fish count (id="fishCount")
  - Stork state (id="storkState", "perched" / "diving" / "returning")
  - Dive count (id="diveCount")

[State Exposure (Required)]
Update window.__3D_STATE__ every frame:
  - modelLoaded: boolean (whether model loading is complete)
  - fishCount: number (current number of visible fish)
  - storkState: string ("perched" / "diving" / "returning")
  - diveCount: number (number of dives)
  - fishPositions: Array<{x,y,z}> (current position of each fish)
  - storkPosition: { x: number, y: number, z: number } (stork position)

Output a single HTML file. Place this file in the same directory as the assets/ folder and run it through a local HTTP server.
